    "description": "Most dehumidifier listings say a hose is included and stop there. LG's page for the DT501BKR0 goes further and gives the approximate size of the hose in the box: an inside diameter of about 0.56 inches, an outside diameter of 0.64 inches and a length of about 4.9 feet. That is useful, because it tells you a five-foot run to a floor drain will work out of the box, and that anything longer will need a replacement hose or an adapter to a garden hose. Retailers list a drain hose and adapter in the box. The unit drains by gravity only; Sylvane's listing confirms there is no internal condensate pump, so the drain needs to sit below the port. Capacity is listed in the ENERGY STAR record at 49.81 pints per day under the current DOE test, with an efficiency of 2.01 liters per kWh and annual energy use of 524 kWh. LG lists a 14.3 pint tank and quoted noise of about 40 dB, and the unit connects to LG's ThinQ app for remote control and alerts. Sylvane lists low-temperature operation rated around 41°F and a 1-year parts warranty. It is a newer model, so stock can be patchy."
  },
  {
    "id": "frigidaire-fhdd5034y1",
    "name": "Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1 50-Pint Dehumidifier with Wi-Fi",
    "category": "50 Pints/Day (2019 DOE), Garden Hose Port, 2.7 Gallon Bucket",
    "badge": "Best Garden Hose Model",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Continuous drain port takes a standard garden hose",
      "2.7 gallon bucket, large for a conventional design",
      "Rated to run down to 41°F room temperature",
      "ENERGY STAR with a listed integrated energy factor of 2.01 L/kWh"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No hose listed in the box; Frigidaire sells a 12-foot kit separately",
      "1-year warranty"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best choice if you already own a garden hose or need a long run: the standard thread means any length you like, from a short leader to 25 feet.",
    "description": "A garden hose connection sounds like a downgrade from an included hose, but for a lot of basements it is the better design. A standard garden hose comes in any length, is easy to replace and threads on with a washer that seals properly, where a short push-on tube often has to be extended with fittings. The FHDD5034Y1 takes that route: its continuous drain port accepts a garden hose, and Frigidaire sells a universal dehumidifier drain kit, the 18FFDHMH01, with a 12-foot cut-to-length hose if you want one made for the job. Frigidaire rates the unit at 50 pints per day under the 2019 DOE test. The spec sheet lists a 2.7 gallon bucket with auto shut-off, three fan speeds with published noise of 47, 49 and 51 dBA, low-temperature operation down to 41°F, 470 watts at 8.8 amps and an integrated energy factor of 2.01 liters per kWh. The cabinet is about 24.7 inches tall and weighs around 41 pounds on casters. Wi-Fi control through the Frigidaire app adds schedules and humidity targets, plus Alexa and Google Home. Like every gravity unit here, it has no pump, so the hose must run downhill."
  },
  {
    "id": "midea-mad50c1aws-a",
    "name": "Midea 50-Pint Dehumidifier MAD50C1AWS-A",
    "category": "49.74 Pints/Day (ENERGY STAR), Standard Hose Socket, No Pump",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "ENERGY STAR record lists 49.74 pints a day and 525 kWh a year",
      "Built-in socket takes a standard hose for continuous drainage",
      "Usually the least expensive 50 pint unit here",
      "Built-in timer and four wheels"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Hose not included, per Midea's own spec page",
      "No Wi-Fi and a 1-year warranty"
