  },
  {
    "id": "midea-cube-35-mad35s1qwt",
    "name": "Midea Cube 35-Pint Dehumidifier MAD35S1QWT",
    "category": "35 Pints/Day, Drain Hose Included, 4.2 Gallon Tank",
    "badge": "Best for Smaller Spaces",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Drain hose included, per Lowe's and Sylvane listings",
      "Same large expanding tank as the 50 pint Cube, about 4.2 gallons",
      "Compact cube shape for a laundry room or finished basement corner",
      "App control"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "35 pints is too small for a large or wet basement",
      "1-year warranty"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The hose-included Cube for a smaller damp space, such as a laundry room, a finished basement room or a storage area beside a floor drain.",
    "description": "Not every space with a floor drain needs a 50 pint dehumidifier. A laundry room, a finished basement bedroom or a storage area beside a utility drain often needs less capacity and less noise, and the 35 pint Cube is the hose-included option at that size. Midea's page says to attach the included hose for continuous drainage to a sink or floor drain, and Lowe's and Sylvane both list a drain hose in the box. Midea rates it at 35 pints per day and markets it for up to 3,500 square feet, a figure we would treat as optimistic. Like its bigger sibling, it uses the lift-and-twist design, and retailers and the manual describe a tank of about 4.2 gallons, very large for this capacity, so if you choose to run it on the tank in a mildly damp room it will go a long time between emptying. Dimensions vary slightly between listings, at roughly 13.5 to 14.5 inches on each side when nested. App control is included. It is not the unit for a large basement with musty walls; for that, the 50 pint Cube or one of the other 50s here is the better fit."
  },
  {
    "id": "alorair-sentinel-hd55s",
    "name": "ALORAIR Sentinel HD55S Crawl Space Dehumidifier",
    "category": "55 PPD at AHAM, Gravity Drain Hose, Rated to 33.8°F",
    "badge": "Best for Crawl Spaces",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Built to drain by gravity, with an external condensate connection",
      "Rated to operate from 33.8°F to 104°F with auto defrost",
      "Can be hung from joists, which adds fall for the drain hose",
      "4.1 amps, 2.4 L/kWh, ENERGY STAR and cETL listed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No bucket at all, so a drain route is mandatory",
      "Listings disagree on the included hose length, from 6.5 to 19.6 feet"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The gravity-drain dehumidifier for a crawl space or unfinished basement corner, where no bucket unit belongs and a hose to a sump or drain is the only sensible plan.",
    "description": "Crawl space and low-grain dehumidifiers are designed around a drain hose rather than a bucket, and the Sentinel HD55S is the most affordable well-specified one we found. AlorAir's spec sheet describes placing it near a drain access with the condensate drained out by gravity, through an external connection point on the cabinet. A seller answer on Amazon describes a 15 mm drain port and a hose of about 14 mm inside diameter, and listings disagree on the hose length in the box, from 6.5 feet on some variants to 19.6 feet on others, so measure your run and check the listing. AlorAir sells a hanging kit that suspends the unit from the joists, which keeps it off a wet floor and also gives the drain hose more fall. Capacity is 55 pints per day at the AHAM condition of 80°F and 60%, and AlorAir lists 34.53 pints per day at 60°F. The rated range is 33.8°F to 104°F with auto defrost, it draws 4.1 amps, delivers 2.4 liters per kWh, weighs 44 pounds and measures 19.2 by 12.2 by 13.3 inches. Ignore the 120 pints in the Amazon title; that is a saturation figure at 90°F and 90% humidity."
  },
  {
    "id": "santa-fe-compact70-crawl-space",
    "name": "Santa Fe Compact70 Dehumidifier (4044110)",
    "category": "70 PPD at 80°F/60%, 8 ft Gravity Hose, 3/4\" NPT Drain",
    "badge": "Best Heavy-Duty Gravity Drain",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Threaded drain connection; Santa Fe's earlier data sheet lists 3/4-inch female NPT",
      "8-foot gravity drain hose in the box, per retailers",
      "Submittal sheet lists 43.7 pints a day at 65°F and 70 at 80°F",
      "Retailers list a 6-year component warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Rated to a 49°F minimum",
      "Priced like professional equipment, well over $1,000"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The drain-hose dehumidifier for someone who wants a permanent, plumbed-style installation that runs for years in a crawl space or mechanical room.",
    "description": "Where most portables use a push-on tube or a garden hose thread, Santa Fe treats the drain like plumbing. The data sheet for the earlier R410A version of the Compact70 lists a 3/4-inch threaded female NPT drain connection and an 8-foot direct gravity drain hose with a 5/8-inch inside diameter, and retailers list an 8-foot hose in the box for the current 4044110 model. A threaded fitting means you can run rigid PVC or a proper condensate line to a floor drain or sump without relying on a hose clamp. Santa Fe's installation guidance calls for a trap in the drain line a short distance from the unit, with the high point of the trap below the drain port, so water cannot back up into the cabinet. Capacity is rated at 70 pints per day at 80°F and 60%, and Santa Fe's submittal sheet lists 43.7 pints per day at 65°F, the current DOE condition. The cabinet is 12 by 12 by 21 inches and 55 pounds, the blower moves 150 CFM, and the operating range is 49°F to 95°F. There is no pump; Santa Fe sells a matching condensate pump with a 20-foot hose for spaces where gravity will not work."
