        <div id="how-gravity-drain-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Dehumidifier Drain Hose Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every compressor dehumidifier collects water in a pan under its coil. On a bucket model, that pan empties into a tank you carry to the sink. On a model with a continuous drain, a port on the cabinet lets the water bypass the tank and run out through a hose instead. There is no pump involved: the water simply flows downhill, so the hose has to slope down from the port to wherever the water is going, the whole way, with no rises in between.
          </p>

          <p>
            That one rule decides almost everything in this guide. Gravity drainage is cheap, silent and has nothing to break, which is why it is the best option whenever it is possible. But it only works if the drain is lower than the dehumidifier's drain port. A floor drain, a sump pit, a basement slab that slopes to a drain, or a crawl space that drains to daylight all work. A laundry sink rim, a standpipe or a basement window above the unit do not, unless you raise the unit high enough or add a pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amount of water involved is the reason to bother. A 50 pint dehumidifier in a damp basement in August can remove 30 to 40 pints a day, which is four to five gallons. Bucket sizes on the units here run from about 14 pints to roughly 4 gallons on the Midea Cube. So a typical bucket fills two or three times a day in summer, and an unemptied bucket switches the dehumidifier off. A drain hose is what turns a dehumidifier from a chore into something that runs unattended for months.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already know your drain is above the unit, skip ahead to our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">dehumidifiers with a pump</a> guide, which covers built-in pumps and lift heights. This page is about the simpler and more reliable case: a dehumidifier, a hose and a drain below it. For the full set of options without a floor drain, see our guide to <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a>. If you are choosing a machine for a large or very damp basement rather than a typical one, our <a href="/best-70-pint-dehumidifier/">70 pint dehumidifier guide</a> explains which units are genuinely bigger under the current rating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="included-vs-garden" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Included Hose vs Garden Hose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dehumidifiers with a drain hose split into two camps. Some ship with a short hose in the box, usually a few feet of flexible tube that pushes or threads onto the drain port. The Midea Cube 50 and 35 and the LG DT501BKR0 are in this group, and LG goes as far as publishing the hose size: about 0.56 inches inside diameter and 4.9 feet long. Others give you a threaded port and leave the hose to you. The Frigidaire FHDD5034Y1 and the conventional Midea MAD50C1AWS-A are in that group, and Midea's spec page says plainly that no hose is included.
          </p>

          <p>
            An included hose is convenient if the drain is close. Plug it in, run it a few feet to a floor drain and you are done. The catch is length. A five-foot hose reaches a drain five feet away in a straight line, and most basements are not that tidy. Extending a push-on tube means a barbed coupling and a second piece of tubing, and every joint is a place for a slow drip. If your drain is more than a few feet away, find out what the included hose connects to before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A garden hose connection solves the length problem. Standard garden hose fittings in North America use a 3/4-inch garden hose thread, and you can buy a hose in any length from a three-foot leader to 50 feet, cut it if you need to, and replace it cheaply when it gets slimy. A threaded fitting with a rubber washer also seals better than a push-on tube. The one thing to check is the washer; a missing or flattened washer is the most common cause of a dripping connection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crawl space units take a third approach. The AlorAir Sentinel HD55S uses an external condensate connection with a hose of about 14 mm inside diameter, according to a seller answer, and the Santa Fe Compact70's earlier data sheet lists a 3/4-inch threaded NPT fitting, which lets you run rigid pipe. For a permanent installation, a threaded fitting and a proper condensate line beat any flexible hose.
          </p>

